00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:41.030
Why is that not defined?

00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:43.280
Yeah?

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:45.560
You can call it if u don't match
the rows of v.

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:47.740
Exactly.
u is 4 by 1, and so is v.

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:53.490
So if I'm trying to apply my
rules of matrix multiplication,

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:56.580
the number of columns of u,

00:11:56.580 --> 00:11:59.240
which is 1, is not equal to the
number of rows of v, which is 4.

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:02.240
So that's not allowed.

00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:08.360
But if I turned u from being a
column vector to a row vector,

00:12:08.860 --> 00:12:11.140
then this would be allowed.

00:12:11.140 --> 00:12:12.360
Do you agree?

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:16.120
because then instead of 4 by 1
here, I'd have a 1 by 4,

00:12:16.120 --> 00:12:20.760
so what operation turns a column
into a row?

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:23.380
It's the transpose.
You can take the transpose of a

00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:24.180
vector,

00:12:24.180 --> 00:12:27.260
and what it gives you is a row
vector, so instead of this

00:12:27.260 --> 00:12:30.540
vector being 4 by 1, it would be
1 by 4.

00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:33.860
You can think of it as a matrix
with one row and four columns.

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:37.040
So if I transpose u,

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:42.100
well then I have a 1 by 4 matrix
being multiplied by a four by

00:12:42.100 --> 00:12:46.360
one matrix, and those fours are
the same, which means it's legal

00:12:46.360 --> 00:12:47.760
to multiply.

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:52.260
And what shape will my output
be?

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:55.520
Will the shape of the output of
this be?

00:12:55.520 --> 00:12:57.740
A one by one matrix.
Yeah, a one by one matrix, which

