01:16:05.010 --> 01:16:08.470
You mean like the amount of
linearly independent vectors is

01:16:08.470 --> 01:16:11.190
the same as the column space?

01:16:11.190 --> 01:16:12.750
That's true.
That's what we're getting to.

01:16:13.030 --> 01:16:15.530
But what I'm asking you is sort
of an aside here.

01:16:15.530 --> 01:16:18.830
All five of these vectors, they
have one algebraic property in

01:16:18.830 --> 01:16:19.830
common.

01:16:22.210 --> 01:16:23.670
Like something about their
components.

01:16:23.670 --> 01:16:25.170
Yeah? Yeah.

01:16:25.870 --> 01:16:27.790
Like each of the vectors,

01:16:27.790 --> 01:16:29.830
like the second column,

01:16:29.830 --> 01:16:31.610
or no, second row element,

01:16:31.610 --> 01:16:34.010
and add it to the third row and
that equals the first.

01:16:34.010 --> 01:16:34.570
Yeah.

01:16:34.570 --> 01:16:38.290
In all of them, it seems like x
equals y plus z.

01:16:38.290 --> 01:16:40.370
5 equals 3 plus 2.

01:16:40.370 --> 01:16:42.670
0 equals negative 1 plus 1.

01:16:42.670 --> 01:16:44.510
3 equals 4 minus 1.

01:16:44.510 --> 01:16:46.230
This shouldn't actually be a
surprise, because if you think

01:16:46.230 --> 01:16:49.250
about the relationship we found
with the columns before,

01:16:49.250 --> 01:16:53.290
we found that a's second column
plus A's third column is equal

01:16:53.290 --> 01:16:56.290
to A's first column.

01:16:56.610 --> 01:16:57.050
Right?

01:16:57.050 --> 01:16:59.850
So we shouldn't be surprised
that there's also dependence

01:16:59.850 --> 01:17:02.530
amongst the rows because we
found dependence amongst the

01:17:02.530 --> 01:17:03.530
columns.

01:17:04.350 --> 01:17:07.250
And now A transpose's

01:17:07.250 --> 01:17:09.710
rows are A's columns.

01:17:09.710 --> 01:17:12.010
Why? Like what does it have to
do with this?

01:17:12.010 --> 01:17:14.610
Because here I was asking you
about A transpose's columns.

01:17:14.610 --> 01:17:17.190
It's because all five of the the

01:17:17.190 --> 01:17:22.830
row vectors of A

01:17:22.830 --> 01:17:28.830
satisfy x

01:17:28.830 --> 01:17:33.330
minus y minus z equals 0.

01:17:33.950 --> 01:17:37.610
Now, why did I write it this
way?

01:17:37.610 --> 01:17:41.190
x minus y minus z equals 0.
What is that the equation of?

01:17:41.190 --> 01:17:41.970
Yeah.

01:17:41.970 --> 01:17:46.470
Yeah, it's the equation of a
plane in R3.

01:17:51.330 --> 01:17:55.630
What this is telling me is all
five of A's rows all live on the

01:17:55.630 --> 01:17:57.830
same plane in R3.

01:17:57.830 --> 01:18:03.080
Which means they all live in a
two-dimensional subspace of R3.

01:18:03.710 --> 01:18:07.410
What that tells you is the
dimension of the column space of

01:18:07.410 --> 01:18:09.370
A transpose, which is the row
space of A,

01:18:09.370 --> 01:18:10.590
is two.

01:18:10.590 --> 01:18:12.730
So the main punchline here,

01:18:12.730 --> 01:18:16.390
and I've kind of just done this
as a proof by example, but,

01:18:16.390 --> 01:18:17.230
um,

01:18:17.230 --> 01:18:21.510
or lots of examples because it
was also true here.

01:18:21.510 --> 01:18:26.710
The main punch line here is that
for any

01:18:26.710 --> 01:18:28.210
matrix A,

01:18:28.630 --> 01:18:32.380
the dimension of its column
space

01:18:34.050 --> 01:18:39.300
is equal to the dimension of its
row space.

01:18:41.530 --> 01:18:44.990
And both of those are equal to
the rank of the matrix.

01:18:44.990 --> 01:18:47.270
So you can think of the rank as
the number of linearly

01:18:47.270 --> 01:18:50.350
independent columns, or also as
the number of linearly

01:18:50.350 --> 01:18:51.850
independent rows.

01:19:13.110 --> 01:19:17.370
Maybe I'll write this up here.

01:19:17.370 --> 01:19:20.390
The dimension of the column
space of A is always equal to

01:19:20.390 --> 01:19:23.350
the dimension of the column
space of A transpose.

01:19:23.350 --> 01:19:26.470
What that tells you is that the
rank of A is also equal to the

01:19:26.470 --> 01:19:28.720
rank of what?

01:19:31.170 --> 01:19:32.670
A transpose.

01:19:33.290 --> 01:19:35.730
A and A transpose always have
the same rank,

01:19:35.730 --> 01:19:38.670
and the number of linearly
independent columns and rows is

01:19:38.670 --> 01:19:40.870
always the same in a matrix,

01:19:40.870 --> 01:19:41.870
which

01:19:42.970 --> 01:19:45.770
I think is a cool result.
If you're coming into class

01:19:45.770 --> 01:19:46.430
today,

01:19:46.430 --> 01:19:50.180
there's no reason necessarily to
believe that that would be true.

01:19:50.550 --> 01:19:51.930
But it is.
The number of linearly

01:19:51.930 --> 01:19:55.680
independent rows and columns in
a matrix is always the same.

01:19:56.890 --> 01:19:59.140
So really quickly,

01:20:00.370 --> 01:20:04.120
