columns?

01:20:10.730 --> 01:20:15.230
Yeah, this would be a tall
matrix.

01:20:15.810 --> 01:20:20.030
Could this matrix's columns all
be linearly independent?

01:20:23.890 --> 01:20:26.150
Could its columns? Its columns
could be,

01:20:26.150 --> 01:20:27.150
right?

01:20:27.330 --> 01:20:28.210
You could just imagine,

01:20:28.210 --> 01:20:28.670
like,

01:20:28.670 --> 01:20:32.610
pretend you have the vector 1,
0, 0, 0 and 0, 0, 0, 1.

01:20:32.610 --> 01:20:34.110
That's a tall matrix.
Its columns are linearly

01:20:34.110 --> 01:20:35.110
independent.

01:20:35.250 --> 01:20:40.690
The columns could all be
linearly

01:20:40.690 --> 01:20:41.690
independent.

01:20:43.190 --> 01:20:46.190
But can it's rows?

01:20:46.190 --> 01:20:49.190
No, it's rows cannot.

01:20:51.810 --> 01:20:52.530
Why?

01:20:52.530 --> 01:20:53.530
Well,

01:20:53.850 --> 01:20:56.150
its number of linearly
independent rows and columns

01:20:56.150 --> 01:20:57.390
have to be the same.

01:20:57.390 --> 01:21:00.410
The max number of linearly
independent columns is D,

01:21:00.410 --> 01:21:02.890
but if D is less than N, that
means the rows can't all be

01:21:02.890 --> 01:21:06.030
linearly independent because for
them to be linearly independent

01:21:07.090 --> 01:21:09.910
the rank of the matrix would
have to be N, but the rank has

01:21:09.910 --> 01:21:15.910
to be to be less than or equal
to the smaller of these two.

01:21:20.910 --> 01:21:25.830
And this is nothing new.

01:21:26.170 --> 01:21:29.730
We said that the dimension of
the subspace spanned

01:21:29.730 --> 01:21:32.310
by a collection of vectors is
less than or equal to the min of

01:21:32.310 --> 01:21:33.090
n and d.

01:21:33.090 --> 01:21:35.990
The rank is just the dimension
of the subspace spanned by the

01:21:35.990 --> 01:21:39.010
columns or the rows of the
matrix.

